// Lesson 1.3 — Your First Script: Variables & Update
// First-introduction lesson: FULL scaffold (walkthrough level 1).
// The walkthrough gives PlayerLogger.cs complete and explains every line,
// because this is the first script these students have ever written.
//
// This lesson also owns the concept that everything downstream depends on:
// SCOPE AND LIFETIME — where you declare a variable decides whether it can
// remember anything between frames. 1.4's movement bug (declaring the input
// variable inside Update) is a symptom of this concept not landing here.
//
// STRUCTURE (tightening pass): the clicks — create the file, attach it, open
// the Console, press Play — are "In the Editor"; the file half is "In the
// Code": the scope rule, the lifecycle API table, the socratic, then the
// walkthrough (whose code blocks open with comments saying where in
// PlayerLogger.cs each piece goes) and the bug hunt.
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION (the scope rule was being explained four times):
//   - the "In the Code" framing paragraph ("the clicks only decide where the
//     script lives...") — said the next block's job instead of doing it;
//   - the "Why this bites so hard" paragraph — restated the scope rule and the
//     Update() table row;
//   - the "How to decide, every time" callout — a callout repeating the
//     paragraph above it with a <strong> lead;
//   - the broken/working counter comparison — a third explanation of scope,
//     after the rule and before the walkthrough line the student is typing.
// Scope is now said twice: once in the rule above the table, once in the
// walkthrough line where the student types it. The bug hunt is the hands-on
// application, not a third explanation.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so the surviving socratic is the
// FIRST of the two committed blocks (sc1) with its first two questions
// untouched and a new synthesis question in the third slot; the second block
// (sc2, which asked the scope question in new words) is deleted whole rather
// than renumbered.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderPane } from '../../lib/panes';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to the first script these students
// ever write. Level 1, so every part is given with what it does AND where to
// read about it — nobody has split a problem before, so the first split is
// demonstrated rather than asked for. The four parts are the walkthrough's
// four steps in order: a file Unity will run, the state that has to be
// remembered, the once-only setup, and the every-frame gate. Part two is the
// scope rule the lesson turns on; part four is where the interval lives.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'Get a script of my own printing to the Console, and keep printing once a second instead of ' +
    'sixty times, so the Console is still readable a minute later.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Make the file something Unity will run',
      does: 'One class in the file, its name matching the filename exactly, inheriting `MonoBehaviour` so Unity can attach it as a component. Get this wrong and the file never runs.',
      doc: 'the `class Boss : MonoBehaviour` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Put the two values that have to be remembered in the class body',
      does: 'A `reportDelay` tuning knob and a `nextReportTime` deadline, both declared outside every method: created once and kept for the whole game, where the same lines inside a method would be rebuilt sixty times a second.',
      doc: 'the declare-vs-assign split in the scope rule below, and the `[SerializeField]` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Give the deadline its first value in the once-only setup',
      does: 'The once-only setup runs before the first frame. It sets `nextReportTime` to the current game time plus the delay, and prints one line so you know the script ran.',
      doc: 'the once-only setup and `Time.time` rows in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Gate the print and re-arm the deadline',
      does: 'The every-frame method runs far too often to print. The `if` asks whether the deadline has passed; if it has, print, then push the deadline a second into the future so the gate closes again.',
      doc: 'the `Time.time` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Only the last part does anything you can see. The first three exist because of one question ' +
    '— does anything next frame need to know about this? — asked three times.',
  sizeTest:
    'Could you look this part up on its own, in one search? "Print to the Console" cannot be ' +
    'looked up; "the seconds since Play" can.',
  turns: [
    'Which of the four parts would you have written first if nobody had shown you the split — and ' +
      'what would have gone wrong?',
  ],
};

// Every code block opens with a comment saying where in PlayerLogger.cs it
// goes. The file is new and built top to bottom, so the placement lines are the
// order of the file itself: the class body, then the fields, then Start(), then
// Update().
const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I print text to the console?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerLogger.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro: 'Type these in order — the whole file is under twenty lines.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Name the file and the class',
      lead: 'A C# file holds one class, and the class name has to match the filename exactly — Unity uses the filename to find your class.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// Assets/Scripts/PlayerLogger.cs — a new, empty file you create in the Project window.', what: '' },
        { code: 'using UnityEngine;', what: 'Tells C# where to find everything Unity provides — `MonoBehaviour`, `Debug`, `Time`. Without it, those names do not exist.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: 'public class PlayerLogger : MonoBehaviour', what: 'Declares the class. The `: MonoBehaviour` part makes it a **component** — a script Unity can attach to a GameObject.', why: 'The name after `class` must match the filename exactly, capitals included, or Unity throws an error the moment you press Play.' },
        { code: '{', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Put the values that have to be remembered in the class body',
      lead: 'Both lines sit inside the class braces but **outside any method** — the placement the scope rule above is about.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerLogger.cs — class body: inside the class braces, outside every method.', what: '' },
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private float reportDelay = 1.0f;', what: 'Seconds between reports. `[SerializeField]` makes a private field editable in the Inspector; the `f` suffix marks it as a `float`, not a `double`.' },
        { code: '    private float nextReportTime;', what: 'When the next report is allowed. `private`: no other script may touch it.', why: 'It has **no value yet**. Declaring and assigning are two separate jobs, and `Start()` is about to do the second one.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Write Start() — the once-only setup',
      lead: '`Start()` is a lifecycle method: Unity calls it for you, exactly once, before the first frame.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerLogger.cs — below the fields, above Update().', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Start()', what: 'The method Unity runs once when the object appears. `void` means it hands nothing back.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;', what: 'The deadline for the first report: current game time plus one second. This is the **assignment** the declaration above left undone.' },
        { code: '        Debug.Log("PlayerLogger is alive.");', what: 'Prints one line to the Console so you know the script attached and ran.' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Write Update() — the every-frame gate',
      lead: '`Update()` runs once per frame — roughly 60 times a second, far too often to print. The `if` is what keeps the Console readable.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerLogger.cs — below Start(). Unity calls this once per frame.', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Update()', what: 'The method Unity runs every frame, automatically.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (Time.time >= nextReportTime)', what: 'The gate: "has enough time passed to report again?" `>=` means *at or past*; the `==` you might expect would be wrong, because frames are not exact.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            Debug.Log("Player has been running for " + Time.time + " seconds.");', what: 'Prints the message. The `+` glues text and number into one string.' },
        { code: '            nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;', what: 'Moves the deadline a second into the future, so the gate closes again.', why: 'Delete this line and the Console floods: the gate stays open, so it prints on every frame from then on.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerLogger : MonoBehaviour
{
    // ---- class body: every field lives up here, outside every method --------
    [SerializeField] private float reportDelay = 1.0f;

    private float nextReportTime;

    // ---- Start(): Unity calls this once, before the first frame ------------
    void Start()
    {
        nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;
        Debug.Log("PlayerLogger is alive.");
    }

    // ---- Update(): Unity calls this every frame ----------------------------
    void Update()
    {
        if (Time.time >= nextReportTime)
        {
            Debug.Log("Player has been running for " + Time.time + " seconds.");
            nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;
        }
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Do not paste it in — compare it against what you built, and be able to say what any differing line would do instead.',
};

const body = `<h1>1.3 — Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Today you write your first C#: one throwaway script, <code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>, that prints to the Console once a second. It teaches the three things every later lesson leans on — how a script attaches to a GameObject, what <code>Start()</code> and <code>Update()</code> do, and where a value has to live to be remembered.</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I print text to the console?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('1.3', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span><span class="activity-time">~15 min</span></div>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts</code>, right-click → Create → C# Script, name it <strong>PlayerLogger</strong>. Unity fills the new file with a class of the same name.</li>
        <li><strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select the <strong>Player</strong>, then <strong>Inspector → Add Component →</strong> search <code>PlayerLogger</code> and add it. Dragging the script file onto the object in the Hierarchy does the same thing.</li>
        <li><strong>Console window → Window → General → Console.</strong> Every log line lands here. Turn on <strong>Clear on Play</strong> so each run starts empty.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong> and watch the Console count the seconds, then press <strong>Play</strong> again to stop — stopping wipes the Console too.</li>
        <li><strong>Inspector →</strong> with the Player selected, find <strong>Player Logger (Script)</strong> and change the <strong>Report Delay</strong> box while the game runs. That box is the private field, tuned without touching code.
${renderPane({
    pane: 'Inspector',
    subject: 'Player',
    caption:
        'The Player\'s Inspector once step 2 is done. `Player Logger (Script)` is a component like ' +
        'any other, and `Report Delay` is the only box it has — `[SerializeField]` is what put a ' +
        'box on a private field.',
    rows: [
        { kind: 'component', label: 'Transform', tick: false },
        { kind: 'component', label: 'Player Controller (Script)' },
        { kind: 'component', label: 'Player Logger (Script)' },
        { kind: 'field', label: 'Report Delay', value: '1', step: 5 },
    ],
})}
        </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>A script on no object never runs.</strong> Skip step 2 and the Console stays empty, with no error to tell you why — so check the Inspector's component list before hunting a bug in the code.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Code</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p><strong>The scope rule:</strong> a variable declared <em>inside a method</em> is created when the method starts and destroyed when it ends, so it cannot remember anything between calls. One declared <em>in the class body</em>, outside every method, belongs to the object and lives as long as the object does. Declaring says the name exists, assigning gives it a value, and one line may do both only when nothing next frame needs the value.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it is</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Start()</code></td><td>Lifecycle hook Unity calls once, before the first frame.</td><td>One-time setup: a field's starting value, one line proving the script ran.</td><td>It runs once, so it cannot watch anything that changes while the game runs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Update()</code></td><td>Lifecycle hook Unity calls every frame — roughly 60 times a second.</td><td>Continuous work: polling input, checking a timer, moving something.</td><td>Not a fixed rate — a heavy frame changes how often it runs, so nothing inside may assume one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Debug.Log(object message)</code></td><td>Prints a value into the Console.</td><td>Developer eyes: with no UI yet, the only way to see what the code is doing.</td><td>A real side effect — one log inside <code>Update()</code> prints sixty messages a second.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Time.time</code></td><td>A <code>float</code>: seconds elapsed since you pressed Play.</td><td>A clock to compare against: "has my deadline passed?"</td><td>It resets when you stop, and keeps counting while paused.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>[SerializeField] private float reportDelay = 1.0f;</code></td><td>A field declaration plus the attribute that serializes it.</td><td>A private field with an Inspector box, tunable without touching code.</td><td>The box beats the code default once set, and exists only on the object carrying the script.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Lifecycle and Timing</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If your computer runs the game at 120 FPS, how many times will <code>Update()</code> execute in one second? On a battery-saving laptop at 30 FPS?</li>
            <li>Write <code>Debug.Log("hello")</code> directly inside <code>Update()</code> with no check around it: what happens to the Console in 5 seconds?</li>
            <li>Ask the scope question about your own script — does anything next frame need to know about this? Which parts of the Break It Down block answer <em>yes</em>, and which answer <em>no</em>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Break the Logger on Purpose</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Cut <code>private float nextReportTime;</code> from the class body and declare it inside the method that runs every frame, giving it a starting value there. What does the Console do now?</li>
            <li>Rename the class to <code>playerLogger</code> (lowercase p), leaving the file named <code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>. What error appears when you press Play?</li>
            <li>Attach the script to the Turret instead of the Player. Does it still run, and what does that prove about components and GameObjects?</li>
            <li>Comment out <code>nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;</code> inside the <code>if</code>. What happens to the Console within 2 seconds, and why does the gate never close again?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Create a C# script, attach it to a GameObject, and read its output in the Console</li>
        <li>Explain which lifecycle method runs once and which runs every single frame</li>
        <li><strong>Explain, without looking, why a variable declared inside a method cannot remember anything — and where it should go instead</strong></li>
        <li>Split a declaration from an assignment, and say why you sometimes must</li>
        <li>Use <code>Time.time</code> to run something on an interval instead of every frame</li>
    </ul>
</div>

